s \u003d new Add(Source.EMPTY, rhs, new Literal(Source.EMPTY, 1L, DataType.LONG));\n                 }\n-                yield EvalMapper.toEvaluator(FOLD_CONTEXT, new Case(Source.EMPTY, condition, List.of(lhs, rhs)), layout(f1, f2))\n-                    .get(driverContext);\n+                EvalOperator.ExpressionEvaluator evaluator \u003d EvalMapper.toEvaluator(\n+                    FOLD_CONTEXT,\n+                    new Case(Source.EMPTY, condition, List.of(lhs, rhs)),\n+                    layout(f1, f2)\n+                ).get(driverContext);\n+                String desc \u003d operation.endsWith(\"lazy\") ? \"CaseLazyEvaluator\" : \"CaseEagerEvaluator\";\n+                if (evaluator.toString().contains(desc) \u003d\u003d false) {\n+                    throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Evaluator was [\" + evaluator + \"] but expected one containing [\" + desc + \"]\");\n+                }\n+                yield evaluator;\n+            }\n+            case \"coalesce_2_noop\", \"coalesce_2_eager\", \"coalesce_2_lazy\" -\u003e {\n+                FieldAttribute f1 \u003d longField();\n+                FieldAttribute f2 \u003d longField();\n+                Expression lhs \u003d f1;","submitted_line":163},"generatedCommentsNodes":[{"comment":"In the `evaluator` method, the check for the evaluator\u0027s description is done using `toString().contains(desc)`. This approach can be fragile and may lead to false negatives if the `toString()` implementation changes. Consider using a more robust way to verify the type of the evaluator, such as checking its class directly.","nodes":[{"id":"benchmarks/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/benchmark/compute/operator/EvalBenchmark.java-benchmarks/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/benchmark/compute/operator/EvalBenchmark.java-6353:6499-6520:6769,6786:6872,6889:7094,7117:7126,7154:7218,7219:8161","promptId":"#PG2FM","srcPath":"benchmarks/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/benchmark/compute/operator/EvalBenchmark.java","dstPa